###### Abstract

We present the results of our multi-wavelength (X-ray to radio) follow-up campaign of the _Einstein Probe_ transient EP240408a. The initial 10 s trigger displayed bright soft X-ray (0.5-4 keV) radiation with peak luminosity L_{\\rm X}\\,\\hbox{\\hbox{$>$}\\kern-8.0pt\\lower 4.3pt\\hbox{$\\sim$}}\\,10^{49} (10^{50}) erg s{}^{-1} for an assumed redshift z\\,\\hbox{\\hbox{$>$}\\kern-8.0pt\\lower 4.3pt\\hbox{$\\sim$}}\\,0.5 (2.0). The _Neil Gehrels Swift Observatory_ and _Neutron star Interior Composition ExploreR_ discovered a fading X-ray counterpart lasting for \\sim5 d (observer frame), which showed a long-lived (\\sim4 d) plateau-like emission (t^{-0.5}) before a sharp powerlaw decline (t^{-7}). The plateau emission was in excess of L_{\\rm X}\\,\\hbox{\\hbox{$>$}\\kern-8.0pt\\lower 4.3pt\\hbox{$\\sim$}}\\,10^{46} (10^{47}) erg s{}^{-1} at z\\,\\hbox{\\hbox{$>$}\\kern-8.0pt\\lower 4.3pt\\hbox{$\\sim$}}\\,0.5 (2.0). Deep optical and radio observations resulted in non-detections of the transient. Our observations with Gemini South revealed a faint potential host galaxy (r\\,\\approx 24 AB mag) near the edge of the X-ray localization. The faint candidate host, and lack of other potential hosts (r\\,\\hbox{\\hbox{$>$}\\kern-8.0pt\\lower 4.3pt\\hbox{$\\sim$}}\\,26 AB mag; J\\,\\hbox{\\hbox{$>$}\\kern-8.0pt\\lower 4.3pt\\hbox{$\\sim$}}\\,23 AB mag), implies a higher redshift origin (z\\,\\hbox{\\hbox{$>$}\\kern-8.0pt\\lower 4.3pt\\hbox{$\\sim$}}\\,0.5), which produces extreme X-ray properties that are inconsistent with many known extragalactic transient classes. In particular, the lack of a bright gamma-ray counterpart, with the isotropic-equivalent energy (10-10,000 keV) constrained by _GECam_ and _Konus-Wind_ to E_{\\rm\\gamma,iso}\\,\\hbox{\\hbox{$<$}\\kern-8.0pt\\lower 4.3pt\\hbox{$\\sim$}}\\,4 \\times 10^{50} (6\\times 10^{51}) erg at z\\,\\approx 0.5 (2.0), conflicts with known gamma-ray bursts (GRBs) of similar X-ray luminosities. We therefore favor a jetted tidal disruption event (TDE) as the progenitor of EP240408a at z\\,\\hbox{\\hbox{$>$}\\kern-8.0pt\\lower 4.3pt\\hbox{$\\sim$}}\\,1.0, possibly caused by the disruption of a white dwarf by an intermediate mass black hole. The alternative is that EP240408a may represent a new, previously unknown class of transient.

